- How would you measure per-route latency and isolate failures to a single route?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a runtime-extendable pipeline, ensuring immutability, async handling, and per-transform error reporting. It also exercises runtime registration and ordering without redeploys.

## Key Concepts

- Registry pattern and dynamic lookup
- Pipeline composition with explicit order
- Immutability and async error handling
- Observability of per-transform failures

## Code Example

```javascript
interface Transformer<T> {
  name: string
  apply(input: T): Promise<T>
}
class TransformerRegistry<T> {
  private map = new Map<string, Transformer<T>>()
  register(t: Transformer<T>): void { this.map.set(t.name, t) }
  get(name: string): Transformer<T> | undefined { return this.map.get(name) }
}
class Pipeline<T> {
  constructor(private reg: TransformerRegistry<T>, private order: string[]) {}
  async run(input: T): Promise<{out: T; errors: string[]}> {
    let acc = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(input)) as T
    const errors: string[] = []
    for (const name of this.order) {
      const t = this.reg.get(name)
      if (!t) continue
      try { acc = await t.apply(acc) }
      catch (e) { errors.push(name + ': ' + (e?.message ?? 'err')) }
    }
    return { out: acc, errors }
  }
}
```

